53`, `:59`, `:65`. |\n19: | Self-certification only | No | The plan names concrete CLI integration tests, provider protocol capture tests, live runtime artifacts, sidecar DB artifacts, source schema files, and scoped compatibility tests rather than treating the plan text itself as validation. |\n20: \n21: ## Findings\n22: | Finding ID | Severity | Runtime claim | Proof plan ref | Proof method | Proxy class | Required runtime artifact | Evidence refs | Blocks pipeline |\n23: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n24: | None | None | No proof-risk finding. | N/A | N/A | N/A | N/A | N/A | No |\n25: \n26: ## Evidence-class decision\n27: \n28: The proof-plan structure is complete. Each claim has a runtime claim, proof method, and evidence-class match statement.\n29: \n30: The external wake and delivery-confirmation claims at `proposal.md:13-29` are runtime/sidecar-path claims. Their proof methods bind to CLI/external-provider integration tests that launch the runner through the external provider fixture, assert sidecar pid-identity/mailbox state, and cover no-turn, submitted-turn marker, mismatch, failure, rate-limit, release, and retry paths. The contract declares `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs` as the CLI launch/notify/wake/resume integration surface and declares `crates/oulipoly-state/src/mailbox.rs` as the intrinsic sidecar mailbox delivery state surface, so the evidence class matches the claim rather than only a detached proxy.\n31: \n32: The provider policy/launch shaping claim at `proposal.md:31-35` targets request and environment construction at the runner/provider boundary. The proof method uses provider-client/executor integration fixtures that capture the actual policy and launch protocol requests, settings identity, launch shape, host linkage env, account auth context, secret filtering, and rejection branch. Because the claim is about protocol request shape and environment boundary, fixture-captured protocol evidence is the matching evidence class, not a proxy mismatch.\n33: \n34: The detached wake registry/session-root claim at `proposal.md:37-41` is bound to CLI/external-provider integration with isolated model/config/data roots and an ingested-session fallback case. That exercises the resume path's model root and session source behavior named by the claim.\n35: \n36: The S10 compatibility claim at `proposal.md:43-47` is mixed evidence with explicit scoping. Deterministic CLI integration tests remain the binding resume-compatibility proof, while live smoke files are scoped only to external launch success. That scoping avoids relying on live smoke as a proxy for resume continuity.\n37: \n38: The xhigh external route and live S11 wake-delivery claims at `proposal.md:49-59` are runtime-artifact-bound and are matched to live runtime task output, argv logs, sidecar `pid-identity.db`, invocation result, exported session, and workload marker files. These are direct runtime/sidecar artifacts for the behavior asserted.\n39: \n40: The no-`state.db` migration claim at `proposal.md:61-65` is a source/schema invariant claim, not a claim that a new production migration executes. The proof method names touched-file inventory, schema and migration source files, and state/mailbox tests showing the behavior uses existing session-turn reads and existing sidecar mailbox columns. The contract also states that no `state.db` schema migration is declared or required, which matches this evidence class.\n41: \n42: ## Residual ambiguity / stop-condition notes\n43: \n44: No stop condition fired. The relative evidence log paths `planning/s11-gate/evidence/runtime-tests.log` and `planning/s11-gate/evidence/live-smoke.md` were not present under `/tmp/s11-gate-worktree-head` during this read-only audit, but the operator scores proof-plan evidence-class matching, and the proposal directly names concrete tests and live artifact paths in the proof methods.\n45: \n46: LOW\n\n(End of file - total 46 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"previe