efs | Blocks pipeline |\n |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n | None | None | No proof-risk finding. | N/A | N/A | N/A | N/A | N/A | No |\n \n ## Evidence-class decision\n \n The proof-plan structure is complete. Each claim has a runtime claim, proof method, and evidence-class match statement.\n \n-The external wake and delivery-confirmation claims at `proposal.md:13-29` are runtime/sidecar-path claims. Their proof methods bind to CLI/external-provider integration tests that launch the runner through the external provider fixture, assert sidecar pid-identity/mailbox state, and cover no-turn, submitted-turn marker, mismatch, failure, rate-limit, release, and retry paths. The contract declares `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs` as the CLI launch/notify/wake/resume integration surface and declares `crates/oulipoly-state/src/mailbox.rs` as the intrinsic sidecar mailbox delivery state surface, so the evidence class matches the claim rather than only a detached proxy.\n+The external launch, wake, delivery-confirmation, and retry claims at `proposal.md:13-29` are runtime/sidecar-path claims. Their proof methods bind to CLI/external-provider integration tests that launch the runner through the external provider fixture, assert sidecar pid-identity/mailbox state, and cover spawn, no-turn, submitted-turn marker, mismatch, failure, rate-limit, claim release, and retry paths. The contract declares `src-tauri/tests/s11_external_provider_wake.rs` as the CLI launch/notify/wake/resume integration surface and declares `crates/oulipoly-state/src/mailbox.rs` as the intrinsic sidecar mailbox delivery state surface, so the evidence class matches the claim rather than only a detached proxy.\n \n-The provider policy/launch shaping claim at `proposal.md:31-35` targets request and environment construction at the runner/provider boundary. The proof method uses provider-client/executor integration fixtures that capture the actual policy and launch protocol requests, settings identity, launch shape, host linkage env, account auth context, secret filtering, and rejection branch. Because the claim is about protocol request shape and environment boundary, fixture-captured protocol evidence is the matching evidence class, not a proxy mismatch.\n+The external-provider transport rotation claim at `proposal.md:31-35` is a runtime dispatch/pool-selection claim. Its proof method uses the `age246_external_transport_rotation` runtime integration fixture to configure multiple provider accounts, force timeout or unavailable classes, assert retry onto the next account, and assert all-slow bounded terminal behavior. That is matching runtime integration evidence for the claimed rotation boundary.\n \n-The detached wake registry/session-root claim at `proposal.md:37-41` is bound to CLI/external-provider integration with isolated model/config/data roots and an ingested-session fallback case. That exercises the resume path's model root and session source behavior named by the claim.\n+The provider policy/launch shaping claim at `proposal.md:37-41` targets request and environment construction at the runner/provider boundary. The proof method uses provider-client/executor integration fixtures that capture the actual policy and launch protocol requests, settings identity, launch shape, host linkage env, account auth context, secret filtering, and rejection branch. Because the claim is about protocol request shape and environment boundary, fixture-captured protocol evidence is the matching evidence class, not a proxy mismatch.\n \n-The S10 compatibility claim at `proposal.md:43-47` is mixed evidence with explicit scoping. Deterministic CLI integration tests remain the binding resume-compatibility proof, while live smoke files are scoped only to external launch success. That scoping avoids relying on live smoke as a proxy for resume continuity.\n+The detached wake registry/session-root claim at `proposal.md:43-47` is bound to CLI/external-provider integration w